1
00:00:01,519 --> 00:00:04,160
Chapter 55.

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:09,358
He was taken to the Police Court next day, and would have been immediately committed for trial,

3
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:13,519
but that it was necessary to send down for an old officer of the prison-ship from

4
00:00:13,519 --> 00:00:20,358
which he had once escaped, to speak to his identity. Nobody doubted it; but Compeyson,

5
00:00:20,358 --> 00:00:26,277
who had meant to depose to it, was tumbling on the tides, dead, and it happened that there

6
00:00:26,277 --> 00:00:31,038
was not at that time any prison officer in London who could give the required evidence.

7
00:00:32,637 --> 00:00:38,038
I had gone direct to Mr. Jaggers at his private house, on my arrival over night,

8
00:00:38,038 --> 00:00:44,796
to retain his assistance, and Mr. Jaggers on the prisoner’s behalf would admit nothing. It

9
00:00:44,796 --> 00:00:49,957
was the sole resource; for he told me that the case must be over in five minutes when

10
00:00:49,957 --> 00:00:55,559
the witness was there, and that no power on earth could prevent its going against us.

11
00:00:55,559 --> 00:01:01,038
I imparted to Mr. Jaggers my design of keeping him in ignorance of the fate of his wealth.

12
00:01:01,839 --> 00:01:07,237
Mr. Jaggers was querulous and angry with me for having “let it slip through my fingers,” and said

13
00:01:07,237 --> 00:01:12,879
we must memorialise by and by, and try at all events for some of it. But he did not conceal

14
00:01:12,879 --> 00:01:17,476
from me that, although there might be many cases in which the forfeiture would not be exacted,

15
00:01:17,476 --> 00:01:23,637
there were no circumstances in this case to make it one of them. I understood that very well. I was

16
00:01:23,637 --> 00:01:29,917
not related to the outlaw, or connected with him by any recognisable tie; he had put his hand to

17
00:01:29,917 --> 00:01:34,680
no writing or settlement in my favour before his apprehension, and to do so now would be

18
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:42,277
idle. I had no claim, and I finally resolved, and ever afterwards abided by the resolution,

19
00:01:42,277 --> 00:01:47,718
that my heart should never be sickened with the hopeless task of attempting to establish one.

20
00:01:47,718 --> 00:01:52,597
There appeared to be reason for supposing that the drowned informer had hoped for a reward

21
00:01:52,597 --> 00:01:58,796
out of this forfeiture, and had obtained some accurate knowledge of Magwitch’s affairs. When

22
00:01:58,796 --> 00:02:04,796
his body was found, many miles from the scene of his death, and so horribly disfigured that

23
00:02:04,796 --> 00:02:10,597
he was only recognisable by the contents of his pockets, notes were still legible,

24
00:02:10,597 --> 00:02:16,957
folded in a case he carried. Among these were the name of a banking-house in New South Wales,

25
00:02:16,957 --> 00:02:22,680
where a sum of money was, and the designation of certain lands of considerable value. Both

26
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:28,438
these heads of information were in a list that Magwitch, while in prison, gave to Mr. Jaggers,

27
00:02:28,438 --> 00:02:34,237
of the possessions he supposed I should inherit. His ignorance, poor fellow, at last served him;

28
00:02:34,237 --> 00:02:39,557
he never mistrusted but that my inheritance was quite safe, with Mr. Jaggers’s aid.

29
00:02:39,559 --> 00:02:43,758
After three days’ delay, during which the crown prosecution stood over for the production of the

30
00:02:43,758 --> 00:02:49,199
witness from the prison-ship, the witness came, and completed the easy case. He was

31
00:02:49,199 --> 00:02:54,436
committed to take his trial at the next Sessions, which would come on in a month.

32
00:02:54,438 --> 00:02:58,199
It was at this dark time of my life that Herbert returned home one evening,

33
00:02:58,199 --> 00:03:00,917
a good deal cast down, and said,—

34
00:03:01,476 --> 00:03:06,319
“My dear Handel, I fear I shall soon have to leave you.”

35
00:03:06,319 --> 00:03:11,277
His partner having prepared me for that, I was less surprised than he thought.

36
00:03:11,277 --> 00:03:14,957
“We shall lose a fine opportunity if I put off going to Cairo,

37
00:03:14,957 --> 00:03:20,597
and I am very much afraid I must go, Handel, when you most need me.”

38
00:03:20,597 --> 00:03:24,796
“Herbert, I shall always need you, because I shall always love you;

39
00:03:24,796 --> 00:03:28,796
but my need is no greater now than at another time.”

40
00:03:28,796 --> 00:03:30,957
“You will be so lonely.”

41
00:03:30,957 --> 00:03:35,199
“I have not leisure to think of that,” said I. “You know that I am always with

42
00:03:35,199 --> 00:03:39,358
him to the full extent of the time allowed, and that I should be with him all day long,

43
00:03:39,358 --> 00:03:45,078
if I could. And when I come away from him, you know that my thoughts are with him.”

44
00:03:45,078 --> 00:03:47,398
The dreadful condition to which he was brought,

45
00:03:47,398 --> 00:03:52,917
was so appalling to both of us, that we could not refer to it in plainer words.

46
00:03:52,917 --> 00:03:58,637
“My dear fellow,” said Herbert, “let the near prospect of our separation—for, it is

47
00:03:58,637 --> 00:04:05,597
very near—be my justification for troubling you about yourself. Have you thought of your future?”

48
00:04:05,597 --> 00:04:09,917
“No, for I have been afraid to think of any future.”

49
00:04:09,917 --> 00:04:17,718
“But yours cannot be dismissed; indeed, my dear dear Handel, it must not be dismissed.

50
00:04:17,718 --> 00:04:23,519
I wish you would enter on it now, as far as a few friendly words go, with me.”

51
00:04:23,519 --> 00:04:25,038
“I will,” said I.

52
00:04:25,038 --> 00:04:29,116
“In this branch house of ours, Handel, we must have a—”

53
00:04:29,116 --> 00:04:34,078
I saw that his delicacy was avoiding the right word, so I said, “A clerk.”

54
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:38,398
“A clerk. And I hope it is not at all unlikely that he may expand

55
00:04:38,398 --> 00:04:42,319
(as a clerk of your acquaintance has expanded) into a partner. Now,

56
00:04:42,319 --> 00:04:46,597
Handel,—in short, my dear boy, will you come to me?”

57
00:04:46,597 --> 00:04:50,160
There was something charmingly cordial and engaging in the manner in which after

58
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:56,237
saying “Now, Handel,” as if it were the grave beginning of a portentous business exordium,

59
00:04:56,237 --> 00:05:03,237
he had suddenly given up that tone, stretched out his honest hand, and spoken like a schoolboy.

60
00:05:03,237 --> 00:05:09,319
“Clara and I have talked about it again and again,” Herbert pursued, “and the dear little

61
00:05:09,319 --> 00:05:14,436
thing begged me only this evening, with tears in her eyes, to say to you that,

62
00:05:14,438 --> 00:05:19,796
if you will live with us when we come together, she will do her best to make you happy,

63
00:05:19,796 --> 00:05:27,116
and to convince her husband’s friend that he is her friend too. We should get on so well, Handel!”

64
00:05:27,116 --> 00:05:32,796
I thanked her heartily, and I thanked him heartily, but said I could not yet make sure

65
00:05:32,796 --> 00:05:38,237
of joining him as he so kindly offered. Firstly, my mind was too preoccupied to be able to take

66
00:05:38,237 --> 00:05:45,199
in the subject clearly. Secondly,—Yes! Secondly, there was a vague something

67
00:05:45,199 --> 00:05:50,160
lingering in my thoughts that will come out very near the end of this slight narrative.

68
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,000
“But if you thought, Herbert, that you could,

69
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:58,559
without doing any injury to your business, leave the question open for a little while—”

70
00:05:58,559 --> 00:06:03,398
“For any while,” cried Herbert. “Six months, a year!”

71
00:06:03,398 --> 00:06:08,237
“Not so long as that,” said I. “Two or three months at most.”

72
00:06:08,237 --> 00:06:13,116
Herbert was highly delighted when we shook hands on this arrangement, and said he could now take

73
00:06:13,116 --> 00:06:17,277
courage to tell me that he believed he must go away at the end of the week.

74
00:06:17,277 --> 00:06:19,879
“And Clara?” said I.

75
00:06:19,879 --> 00:06:24,680
“The dear little thing,” returned Herbert, “holds dutifully to her father as long as he

76
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:30,637
lasts; but he won’t last long. Mrs. Whimple confides to me that he is certainly going.”

77
00:06:31,957 --> 00:06:37,597
“Not to say an unfeeling thing,” said I, “he cannot do better than go.”

78
00:06:37,597 --> 00:06:42,476
“I am afraid that must be admitted,” said Herbert; “and then I shall come back for the dear little

79
00:06:42,476 --> 00:06:49,319
thing, and the dear little thing and I will walk quietly into the nearest church. Remember! The

80
00:06:49,319 --> 00:06:54,879
blessed darling comes of no family, my dear Handel, and never looked into the red book,

81
00:06:54,879 --> 00:07:00,637
and hasn’t a notion about her grandpapa. What a fortune for the son of my mother!”

82
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:06,078
On the Saturday in that same week, I took my leave of Herbert,—full of bright hope,

83
00:07:06,078 --> 00:07:12,160
but sad and sorry to leave me,—as he sat on one of the seaport mail coaches. I went into

84
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:16,718
a coffee-house to write a little note to Clara, telling her he had gone off,

85
00:07:16,718 --> 00:07:21,957
sending his love to her over and over again, and then went to my lonely home,—if

86
00:07:21,957 --> 00:07:28,000
it deserved the name; for it was now no home to me, and I had no home anywhere.

87
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,116
On the stairs I encountered Wemmick, who was coming down,

88
00:07:31,116 --> 00:07:35,839
after an unsuccessful application of his knuckles to my door. I had not

89
00:07:35,839 --> 00:07:40,637
seen him alone since the disastrous issue of the attempted flight; and he had come,

90
00:07:40,637 --> 00:07:48,160
in his private and personal capacity, to say a few words of explanation in reference to that failure.

91
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:52,758
“The late Compeyson,” said Wemmick, “had by little and little got at the bottom of half of

92
00:07:52,758 --> 00:07:58,358
the regular business now transacted; and it was from the talk of some of his people in trouble

93
00:07:58,358 --> 00:08:04,277
(some of his people being always in trouble) that I heard what I did. I kept my ears open,

94
00:08:04,277 --> 00:08:09,116
seeming to have them shut, until I heard that he was absent, and I thought that would be the

95
00:08:09,116 --> 00:08:15,839
best time for making the attempt. I can only suppose now, that it was a part of his policy,

96
00:08:15,839 --> 00:08:21,597
as a very clever man, habitually to deceive his own instruments. You don’t blame me,

97
00:08:21,597 --> 00:08:27,398
I hope, Mr. Pip? I am sure I tried to serve you, with all my heart.”

98
00:08:27,398 --> 00:08:29,519
“I am as sure of that, Wemmick, as you can be,

99
00:08:29,519 --> 00:08:33,597
and I thank you most earnestly for all your interest and friendship.”

100
00:08:33,597 --> 00:08:36,277
“Thank you, thank you very much. It’s a bad job,” said Wemmick,

101
00:08:36,277 --> 00:08:42,038
scratching his head, “and I assure you I haven’t been so cut up for a long time.

102
00:08:42,038 --> 00:08:47,796
What I look at is the sacrifice of so much portable property. Dear me!”

103
00:08:47,796 --> 00:08:51,879
“What I think of, Wemmick, is the poor owner of the property.”

104
00:08:51,879 --> 00:08:56,116
“Yes, to be sure,” said Wemmick. “Of course, there can be no objection to

105
00:08:56,116 --> 00:09:00,519
your being sorry for him, and I’d put down a five-pound note myself to get him out of it.

106
00:09:01,319 --> 00:09:06,319
But what I look at is this. The late Compeyson having been beforehand with

107
00:09:06,319 --> 00:09:11,398
him in intelligence of his return, and being so determined to bring him to book,

108
00:09:11,398 --> 00:09:17,277
I do not think he could have been saved. Whereas, the portable property certainly could have been

109
00:09:17,277 --> 00:09:23,476
saved. That’s the difference between the property and the owner, don’t you see?”

110
00:09:23,476 --> 00:09:28,680
I invited Wemmick to come upstairs, and refresh himself with a glass of grog before walking to

111
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:35,116
Walworth. He accepted the invitation. While he was drinking his moderate allowance,

112
00:09:35,116 --> 00:09:40,637
he said, with nothing to lead up to it, and after having appeared rather fidgety,—

113
00:09:40,637 --> 00:09:45,559
“What do you think of my meaning to take a holiday on Monday, Mr. Pip?”

114
00:09:45,559 --> 00:09:49,758
“Why, I suppose you have not done such a thing these twelve months.”

115
00:09:49,758 --> 00:09:57,680
“These twelve years, more likely,” said Wemmick. “Yes. I’m going to take a holiday. More than that;

116
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:04,199
I’m going to take a walk. More than that; I’m going to ask you to take a walk with me.”

117
00:10:04,199 --> 00:10:11,839
I was about to excuse myself, as being but a bad companion just then, when Wemmick anticipated me.

118
00:10:11,839 --> 00:10:15,796
“I know your engagements,” said he, “and I know you are out of sorts,

119
00:10:15,796 --> 00:10:21,597
Mr. Pip. But if you could oblige me, I should take it as a kindness. It ain’t a long walk,

120
00:10:21,597 --> 00:10:26,000
and it’s an early one. Say it might occupy you (including breakfast on the

121
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,438
walk) from eight to twelve. Couldn’t you stretch a point and manage it?”

122
00:10:31,277 --> 00:10:36,796
He had done so much for me at various times, that this was very little to do for him. I

123
00:10:36,796 --> 00:10:42,637
said I could manage it,—would manage it,—and he was so very much pleased by my acquiescence,

124
00:10:42,637 --> 00:10:47,879
that I was pleased too. At his particular request, I appointed to call for him at

125
00:10:47,879 --> 00:10:53,957
the Castle at half past eight on Monday morning, and so we parted for the time.

126
00:10:53,957 --> 00:10:58,957
Punctual to my appointment, I rang at the Castle gate on the Monday morning, and was received by

127
00:10:58,957 --> 00:11:06,000
Wemmick himself, who struck me as looking tighter than usual, and having a sleeker hat on. Within,

128
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:11,879
there were two glasses of rum and milk prepared, and two biscuits. The Aged must have been stirring

129
00:11:11,879 --> 00:11:18,519
with the lark, for, glancing into the perspective of his bedroom, I observed that his bed was empty.

130
00:11:18,519 --> 00:11:21,758
When we had fortified ourselves with the rum and milk and biscuits,

131
00:11:21,758 --> 00:11:25,398
and were going out for the walk with that training preparation on us,

132
00:11:25,398 --> 00:11:30,319
I was considerably surprised to see Wemmick take up a fishing-rod, and put it over his shoulder.

133
00:11:31,637 --> 00:11:39,957
“Why, we are not going fishing!” said I. “No,” returned Wemmick, “but I like to walk with one.”

134
00:11:39,957 --> 00:11:44,559
I thought this odd; however, I said nothing, and we set off. We

135
00:11:44,559 --> 00:11:49,957
went towards Camberwell Green, and when we were thereabouts, Wemmick said suddenly,—

136
00:11:49,957 --> 00:11:52,796
“Halloa! Here’s a church!”

137
00:11:52,796 --> 00:11:56,680
There was nothing very surprising in that; but again, I was rather surprised,

138
00:11:56,680 --> 00:12:00,358
when he said, as if he were animated by a brilliant idea,—

139
00:12:00,358 --> 00:12:01,796
“Let’s go in!”

140
00:12:01,796 --> 00:12:07,839
We went in, Wemmick leaving his fishing-rod in the porch, and looked all round. In the mean time,

141
00:12:07,839 --> 00:12:12,680
Wemmick was diving into his coat-pockets, and getting something out of paper there.

142
00:12:12,680 --> 00:12:17,680
“Halloa!” said he. “Here’s a couple of pair of gloves! Let’s put ’em on!”

143
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:22,796
As the gloves were white kid gloves, and as the post-office was widened to its utmost extent,

144
00:12:22,796 --> 00:12:28,000
I now began to have my strong suspicions. They were strengthened into certainty when

145
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:32,038
I beheld the Aged enter at a side door, escorting a lady.

146
00:12:32,038 --> 00:12:36,879
“Halloa!” said Wemmick. “Here’s Miss Skiffins! Let’s have a wedding.”

147
00:12:36,879 --> 00:12:42,078
That discreet damsel was attired as usual, except that she was now engaged in substituting for her

148
00:12:42,078 --> 00:12:47,398
green kid gloves a pair of white. The Aged was likewise occupied in preparing

149
00:12:47,398 --> 00:12:53,160
a similar sacrifice for the altar of Hymen. The old gentleman, however, experienced so

150
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:57,519
much difficulty in getting his gloves on, that Wemmick found it necessary to put him

151
00:12:57,519 --> 00:13:03,277
with his back against a pillar, and then to get behind the pillar himself and pull away at them,

152
00:13:03,277 --> 00:13:08,237
while I for my part held the old gentleman round the waist, that he might present an equal

153
00:13:08,237 --> 00:13:14,917
and safe resistance. By dint of this ingenious scheme, his gloves were got on to perfection.

154
00:13:14,917 --> 00:13:20,917
The clerk and clergyman then appearing, we were ranged in order at those fatal rails. True to

155
00:13:20,917 --> 00:13:26,519
his notion of seeming to do it all without preparation, I heard Wemmick say to himself,

156
00:13:26,519 --> 00:13:33,078
as he took something out of his waistcoat-pocket before the service began, “Halloa! Here’s a ring!”

157
00:13:33,078 --> 00:13:39,637
I acted in the capacity of backer, or best-man, to the bridegroom; while a little limp pew-opener in

158
00:13:39,637 --> 00:13:45,597
a soft bonnet like a baby’s, made a feint of being the bosom friend of Miss Skiffins. The

159
00:13:45,597 --> 00:13:50,879
responsibility of giving the lady away devolved upon the Aged, which led to the clergyman’s being

160
00:13:50,879 --> 00:13:57,116
unintentionally scandalised, and it happened thus. When he said, “Who giveth this woman to

161
00:13:57,116 --> 00:14:02,277
be married to this man?” the old gentleman, not in the least knowing what point of the ceremony

162
00:14:02,277 --> 00:14:08,796
we had arrived at, stood most amiably beaming at the ten commandments. Upon which, the clergyman

163
00:14:08,796 --> 00:14:16,358
said again, “WHO giveth this woman to be married to this man?” The old gentleman being still in

164
00:14:16,358 --> 00:14:22,519
a state of most estimable unconsciousness, the bridegroom cried out in his accustomed voice,

165
00:14:22,519 --> 00:14:29,237
“Now Aged P. you know; who giveth?” To which the Aged replied with great briskness, before saying

166
00:14:29,237 --> 00:14:34,237
that he gave, “All right, John, all right, my boy!” And the clergyman came to so gloomy

167
00:14:34,237 --> 00:14:40,000
a pause upon it, that I had doubts for the moment whether we should get completely married that day.

168
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,879
It was completely done, however, and when we were going out of church Wemmick took the cover off the

169
00:14:44,879 --> 00:14:50,796
font, and put his white gloves in it, and put the cover on again. Mrs. Wemmick, more heedful of the

170
00:14:50,796 --> 00:14:57,718
future, put her white gloves in her pocket and assumed her green. “Now, Mr. Pip,” said Wemmick,

171
00:14:57,718 --> 00:15:02,277
triumphantly shouldering the fishing-rod as we came out, “let me ask you whether anybody

172
00:15:02,277 --> 00:15:07,879
would suppose this to be a wedding-party!” Breakfast had been ordered at a pleasant

173
00:15:07,879 --> 00:15:13,758
little tavern, a mile or so away upon the rising ground beyond the green; and there was a bagatelle

174
00:15:13,758 --> 00:15:19,559
board in the room, in case we should desire to unbend our minds after the solemnity. It

175
00:15:19,559 --> 00:15:24,879
was pleasant to observe that Mrs. Wemmick no longer unwound Wemmick’s arm when it adapted

176
00:15:24,879 --> 00:15:30,319
itself to her figure, but sat in a high-backed chair against the wall, like a violoncello

177
00:15:30,319 --> 00:15:36,199
in its case, and submitted to be embraced as that melodious instrument might have done.

178
00:15:36,199 --> 00:15:42,038
We had an excellent breakfast, and when any one declined anything on table, Wemmick said,

179
00:15:42,038 --> 00:15:49,078
“Provided by contract, you know; don’t be afraid of it!” I drank to the new couple, drank to the

180
00:15:49,078 --> 00:15:56,879
Aged, drank to the Castle, saluted the bride at parting, and made myself as agreeable as I could.

181
00:15:56,879 --> 00:16:03,957
Wemmick came down to the door with me, and I again shook hands with him, and wished him joy.

182
00:16:03,957 --> 00:16:08,957
“Thankee!” said Wemmick, rubbing his hands. “She’s such a manager of fowls,

183
00:16:08,957 --> 00:16:14,277
you have no idea. You shall have some eggs, and judge for yourself. I say,

184
00:16:14,277 --> 00:16:21,358
Mr. Pip!” calling me back, and speaking low. “This is altogether a Walworth sentiment, please.”

185
00:16:21,358 --> 00:16:26,000
“I understand. Not to be mentioned in Little Britain,” said I.

186
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,398
Wemmick nodded. “After what you let out the other day,

187
00:16:29,398 --> 00:16:33,877
Mr. Jaggers may as well not know of it. He might think my brain was softening,

188
00:16:33,879 --> 00:16:36,000
or something of the kind.” Chapter 56.
